---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/marsello-vs-yotpo-email-sms';
const title = 'Marsello vs Yotpo Email & SMS: 2026 Migration Guide';
const desc = 'Yotpo Email & SMS is decommissioned. Compare Marsello retail loyalty, retained Yotpo Reviews and replacement marketing sender ownership for Shopify.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Marsello or Yotpo Email better for a Shopify store with POS?',
    answer: 'For a current evaluation, separate Marsello loyalty from Yotpo Reviews. Yotpo Email & SMS has been decommissioned, so it is not an active suite-consolidation alternative. Evaluate Marsello against the retail ledger job and select an available marketing sender separately.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Marsello vs Yotpo pricing compare?',
    answer: 'Request quotes for the required loyalty sites, Reviews functions and replacement marketing sender. Match contacts, orders, sends, destinations and message segments, then include integration, support, migration and reconciliation labor. An existing Reviews subscription does not establish the marginal cost of a new sender.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better loyalty-triggered email?',
    answer: 'Separate loyalty state from email delivery. Demonstrate a qualifying purchase, tier change and return in the ledger, then trace the event to the supported sender. Compare the actual integration contract and current product availability; a suite label does not prove that a loyalty milestone can safely enqueue a message.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I use Marsello for loyalty and Yotpo for reviews only?',
    answer: 'Assign one loyalty ledger and one review-request sender. Map delivery events, current return state and replay IDs before enabling either workflow. A points confirmation and a review request have different jobs; coordinate their timing and stop old queued requests during migration rather than assuming that a shared customer profile prevents duplicates.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Marsello or Yotpo?',
    answer: 'Consider Sequenzy as another email candidate only if its documented event inputs and cancellation controls meet the migration requirements. It does not replace review moderation or a points ledger simply by replacing the marketing sender.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which platform handles review request email better?',
    answer: 'Evaluate delivered-item eligibility, return exclusions, review submission deduplication and moderation ownership. For visual content, separately record permission and approved channels of reuse. Review collection is a different job from awarding loyalty points, and an existing Reviews account does not establish current email sender availability.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Yotpo SMS match Marsello for retail?',
    answer: 'Yotpo Email & SMS is decommissioned. Preserve any historical SMS permission and suppression provenance for migration, but confirm the destination sender can import and apply it. Review-request emails in Yotpo Reviews are a separate product workflow, not evidence that the former SMS platform remains available.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'For loyalty, test a POS purchase followed by an online return and identity merge. For reviews, test delivered, delayed and returned line items plus a repeated delivery event. Require one correct ledger and one eligible review request, with a named sender and documented content reuse permission.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Marsello vs Yotpo Email & SMS</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Marsello vs Yotpo Email & SMS for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">
          This comparison separates two jobs: reconciling loyalty across retail and online checkout, and collecting reviews with permission to reuse customer content. The candle-retailer, skincare and supplement scenarios below are proposed evaluations, not merchant observations. Yotpo Email & SMS has been decommissioned. This route now addresses a migration decision: preserve the Reviews workflow while evaluating a replacement marketing sender and the appropriate loyalty ledger.
        </p>
        <p>Yotpo's <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">migration announcement</a> scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025; its <a href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">product site</a> now states it is decommissioned. Reviews request email remains a separate documented workflow. Evaluate a replacement sender without assuming all Reviews functions must move.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'POS ledger', tool: 'Marsello', reason: 'Evaluate lookup, earn, return and identity reconciliation.' },
            { label: 'Review collection', tool: 'Yotpo Reviews', reason: 'Keep request eligibility and moderation separate from marketing.' },
            { label: 'Marketing sender', tool: 'Replacement required', reason: 'Yotpo Email & SMS is decommissioned.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Marsello</th><th>Yotpo Email & SMS</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Current decision</td><td>Evaluate a retail loyalty ledger</td><td>Replace the decommissioned marketing sender</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reviews</td><td>Map the selected Reviews integration</td><td>Reviews request email is a separate workflow</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Return handling</td><td>Reconcile earned benefit reversal</td><td>Inspect refunded-item request eligibility</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Identity</td><td>Reconcile POS and online customer joins</td><td>Map order and product IDs to review requests</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating cost</td><td>Include sites, ledger and sender integration</td><td>Include retained Reviews and replacement sender scope</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Why this comparison matters for Shopify merchants</h2>
        <p>
          For a candle retailer, a POS receipt and a later refill order must resolve to the correct loyalty member. For a skincare store, a delivered product must resolve to the correct review request, while any photo used in marketing needs appropriate reuse permission. Those are different acceptance criteria. Compare them separately rather than treating more modules as evidence of a better customer journey.
        </p>
        <p>
          Proposed supplement-store audit: map the delivery event to every review request, points confirmation and marketing follow-up it can enqueue. Record the sender, eligibility rule and cancellation owner for each. Multiple messages are not evidence of damaged trust by themselves; examine purpose, timing, permission and complaints without inventing a merchant outcome.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: candle retailer with four POS locations</h2>
        <p>
          Proposed candle-retailer evaluation: mark a holiday gift purchase separately from a personal refill purchase. Join POS and online records using a reconciled customer identity, then test a return and a merged account. A gift buyer should not automatically enter the recipient refill journey, and a reversed purchase should not continue to qualify the member for an earned benefit.
        </p>
        <p>
          No 31% cross-channel revenue result is established here. Trace a refill order back to eligible loyalty events and the designated sender, while retaining orders that occurred without message exposure. Attribution can describe a reporting rule; incremental refill revenue requires a suitable comparison group and explicit treatment of seasonality and gift purchases.
        </p>

        <h2>Merchant scenario: skincare DTC on Yotpo Reviews</h2>
        <p>Proposed skincare migration: inventory approved customer photos by product, source and permitted reuse channel. Keep review collection and moderation with their assigned owner while mapping approved content to the replacement sender. A visible review widget is not proof that the sender can retrieve the same content or that marketing reuse is authorized.</p>
        <p>Compare the effort of retaining Reviews with a replacement sender against moving the review collection workflow too. Include content mapping, moderation history and permission records in migration scope. No click improvement, savings or merchant observation is established here.</p>

        <h2>Workflow test: post-purchase review request</h2>
        <p>
          Proposed review-request test: use a delivered line item, a delayed shipment and a returned item. Check the request against delivery and return state immediately before sending. Record one request per eligible item or documented order-level policy, and replay the delivery event to check deduplication. No 18% review-conversion result or observed timing is established here.
        </p>
        <p>Yotpo's <a href="https://support.yotpo.com/docs/creating-a-review-request">request setup guide</a> warns that a timing change applies to new orders rather than existing queued orders. Inventory the old queue before changing timing; otherwise a new setting can appear correct while earlier requests still follow the previous schedule.</p>

        <h2>Workflow test: abandoned cart with social proof</h2>
        <p>For a proposed social-proof reminder, use only content approved for the intended channel and mapped to the correct SKU. Suppress the reminder after purchase and remove returned-item eligibility where appropriate. Test missing product mappings and withdrawn content permission. A loyalty balance reminder instead requires current ledger entitlement; these are different data contracts.</p>
        <p>
          A proposed content experiment should compare approved review content with a clearly defined alternative for the same eligible audience. Keep offer, send timing and attribution window comparable, and retain a no-message holdout if estimating incremental orders. No supplement-store A/B result is asserted here.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing reality at growing list sizes</h2>
        <p>Compare matched all-in scope: loyalty sites, Reviews features, replacement sender, contacts, sends, SMS destinations, message segments, carrier charges, support and migration labor. Do not count existing Reviews spending as proof that replacement messaging is cheaper. This page establishes no current price or measured savings.</p>

        <h2>Team fit: who should choose which</h2>
        <p>Choose the retail-ledger evaluation when associates need to explain benefits across POS and online returns. Require a named owner for customer merges and balance disputes. A replacement marketing sender must consume that state without awarding points again on replay.</p>
        <p>Retain the Reviews workflow when its collection and moderation history remains useful. Assign one request sender per eligible item and document how queued requests transfer or remain in place. The marketing operator owns replacement broadcasts and behavioral reminders; the Reviews operator owns moderation, collection status and permitted content reuse.</p>

        <h2>Also consider Sequenzy</h2>
        <p>Sequenzy can remain an additional email candidate if its supported event contract and sender controls satisfy this migration. Keep Reviews and loyalty ownership explicit; an email purchase does not itself migrate content rights, historical review status or points balances.</p>

        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Choose a workflow owner before choosing a suite. The retail test should pass identity reconciliation, return reversal and associate lookup; the reviews test should pass delivery eligibility, one-request ownership and content reuse permission. The former Yotpo marketing sender is decommissioned; keep Reviews request ownership separate from the replacement marketing sender. Resolve the actual event mapping and permission transfer before enabling the replacement.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/marsello-vs-klaviyo">Marsello vs Klaviyo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Klaviyo vs Yotpo Email & SMS</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-yotpo-email-sms">Sequenzy vs Yotpo Email & SMS</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/beauty">Email apps for beauty stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/post-purchase">Post-purchase playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-12">
      <h2>Resolve the request queue before the marketing cutover</h2>
      <p>Yotpo's <a href="https://support.yotpo.com/docs/review-requests-dashboard">Email Status Dashboard guide</a> distinguishes scheduled, sent, not-sent and canceled requests. It documents refund exclusions, including non-refunded items on partially refunded Shopify orders. Use order IDs to reconcile the queue against your own eligible-item inventory; do not infer eligibility from an email being scheduled.</p>
      <p>For the proposed gift-order test, record purchaser and recipient roles separately. A review request belongs to the appropriate product experience; a refill promotion requires its own purpose and channel permission. Preserve suppression provenance when transferring marketing records and identify who cancels waiting messages in the old sender.</p>
      <p>Measure review submission and approved reusable content separately from repeat-purchase revenue. Attribution windows can associate a sale with a message without establishing causal lift. A proposed holdout should account for seasonality and gift orders; no experiment or merchant result is reported here.</p>
    </div>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Marsello vs Yotpo Email & SMS FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
